  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a glass-based optical data storage system with three linked stages: writing, storing, and reading information. In the writing step, a laser is focused into a glass chip to create tiny modified regions, or voxels, arranged in a dense three-dimensional grid. The zoomed in sketches suggest that these written spots encode information through more than one optical property: simple phase changes visible from the top and elongated birefringent features with orientation-dependent shapes. A side-view inset indicates the voxels are also organized through the depth of the glass, emphasizing true volumetric storage rather than a flat surface pattern.\n\nThe storage concept is then shown as many such chips being archived together in a larger rack-like unit, implying high-density, durable data preservation in compact physical form.\n\nFor reading, a microscope camera images the chip and a highlighted block within the voxel array, suggesting that the stored optical signatures can be decoded non-destructively by imaging. Overall, the image conveys a method for writing structured laser-induced nanoscopic features inside glass, keeping the chips as long-term memory media, and later retrieving the data by optical microscopy.",
